PLACES=[
N('fort-fred-steele','Fort Fred Steele','Sinclair','wy','fort','ruins',41.77816,-106.94673,'site','public','walk','forts','1868–1886',
  'The Army set up Fort Fred Steele on June 20, 1868, where the Union Pacific crossed the North Platte, to guard the line being built across southern Wyoming; it was one of three railroad forts with Fort Sanders and Fort D.A. Russell. Grant and Sherman met railroad officials here that summer. Troops from the post were ambushed at Milk Creek during the White River War (Wikipedia gives the year as 1878) and their commander, Major Thomas Thornburgh, was killed. The Army left on August 7, 1886; the site went on as a hub of the lumber and livestock trades, and repeated fires have since taken most of the buildings.',
  'Wyoming State Parks says many foundations can be seen but very few structures are intact. Walk the grounds by the river and the old bridge crossing. Which buildings stand and what is in the museum was not checked from a source opened in this session.',
  'The railroad fort where Grant and Sherman came to look at the Union Pacific.',
  'Fort Fred Steele State Historic Site, run by the Division of State Parks, Historic Sites and Trails. Wyoming State Parks gives it as I-80 exit 228, 1.25 miles north of the rest area; open daily 9 a.m. to 7 p.m. from Memorial Day to Labor Day and closed for the winter after that (page read November 2026). Phone 307-320-3013. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Fort Fred Steele State Historic Site'),L('Wyoming State Parks: About Fort Fred Steele','https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/about-fort-fred-steele')],brick='?'),
N('wyoming-frontier-prison','Wyoming Frontier Prison (old state penitentiary)','Rawlins','wy','inst','standing',41.79293,-107.24234,'exact','open','drive','','1890s–1981',
  'The Wyoming State Penitentiary opened in Rawlins in 1901 in a stone building designed by Walter E. Ware of Salt Lake City and built in stages through the 1890s. Its Romanesque front has a round arch, carved sills, short columns with leafy capitals and cone-roofed towers. The prison moved to a new site outside town in 1981. The 1983 Register district holds 14 buildings, and the old cell house is now a museum.',
  'Inside only on a guided tour, about an hour, which takes in the cell blocks and the gas chamber where executions were carried out; the museum exhibits (including the Wyoming Peace Officers\' Museum) are free. Tours run all year on a schedule that changes with the season.',
  'A frontier penitentiary you can walk through, gas chamber and all.',
  'Wyoming Frontier Prison, 500 W. Walnut Street; parking on Walnut Street by the building. Its page (read November 2026): summer, Memorial Day to Labor Day, tours every hour on the half hour 9:30 to 4:30 seven days a week and the museum 9 to 5; winter, mid-November to Memorial Day, tours at 10:30 and 1:30 Monday to Thursday, museum 9 to 4 with a noon hour, closed winter holidays; closed mid-October to mid-November for its Halloween events; tours cancelled at 0 F or below. Adults $15, children 6-12 and seniors $12. Groups of 10 or more book by phone, 307-324-4422. Leashed pets allowed.',
  [W('Wyoming State Penitentiary'),L('Wyoming Frontier Prison: guided tours','https://www.wyomingfrontierprison.org/explore/guided-tours')],brick='?',hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day: tours hourly on the half hour 9:30 to 4:30, seven days a week. Mid-November to Memorial Day: tours at 10:30 and 1:30, Monday to Thursday'),
N('rawlins-union-pacific-depot','Union Pacific depot, Rawlins','Rawlins','wy','rail','standing',41.78692,-107.23851,'exact','view','drive','stations','1901',
  'The Union Pacific built this depot in 1901 to its own engineering department\'s design: a long one-story brick building with a flared hipped roof on deep brackets, projecting bays, an entrance tower and stepped gables, larger and finer than the usual frame depot. An Eating House of 1916 stood beside it, joined by an arcade. Trains stopped until 1971, then Amtrak in 1971-1983 and 1991-1997. The railroad gave it to the city in the 1990s.',
  'The outside is the thing to see: brick walls, the tower over the entrance, the bays facing the tracks. Wikipedia says the inside was remodelled in the 1940s and lost much of its woodwork, and it is now offices and rental rooms. Interior not open except to people renting it.',
  '',
  'Owned by the City of Rawlins, 400 W. Front Street at 4th Street, beside Depot Park; the city rents its two rooms for events (rawlinswy.gov, read November 2026). See it from the street and the park. The Union Pacific main line beside it is active: stay off the tracks.',
  [W('Rawlins station'),L('City of Rawlins: Depot rental information','https://rawlinswy.gov/278'),L(CCVC+': The Old Union Pacific Train Depot','https://snowmobileinwyoming.com/things-to-do/arts-and-culture/old-union-pacific-train-depot')],brick='yes'),
N('ferris-mansion-rawlins','George Ferris Mansion','Rawlins','wy','landmark','standing',41.79134,-107.24329,'exact','view','drive','','1899–1903',
  'George Ferris made his money from the Rudefeha (later Ferris-Haggarty) copper mine in the Sierra Madre and began this Queen Anne house in 1899 to a design from a Barber and Klutz pattern book. He was killed in 1900 when he was thrown from a runaway carriage near the mine; his widow Julia finished the house in 1903 and lived there until 1931. It was later cut up into apartments and fell into ruin. The Lubbers family bought it in 1979 and spent 25 years restoring it.',
  'A corner turret, Colorado pressed brick in 18-inch walls trimmed with local sandstone, and a carriage house built with it (Cowboy State Daily, 2023). Wikipedia calls it a bed-and-breakfast; a 2025 or 2026 report says it was put up for sale. Whether it takes guests now was not checked: see it from the street.',
  '',
  'Private, 607 W. Maple Street. View from the street. Not open to the public as far as any source opened here says.',
  [W('George Ferris Mansion'),L('Cowboy State Daily: From ruin to regal (2023)','https://cowboystatedaily.com/2023/09/23/from-ruin-to-regal-a-wyoming-familys-mission-to-save-the-ferris-mansion/'),L('Wake Up Wyoming: Ferris Mansion for sale','https://wakeupwyo.com/ferris-mansion-rawlins-wy/')],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('france-memorial-church-rawlins','France Memorial Presbyterian Church','Rawlins','wy','worship','standing',41.78815,-107.23666,'exact','view','drive','','1882',
  'Rawlins\' Presbyterians built their first church in 1870, the only church in town, and outgrew it by 1880. This Gothic Revival church went up in 1882 for $7,800 on a mortgage that James France, husband of a member, largely paid off in 1885; it was named for his wife Elizabeth. It is built of stone quarried in the hills north of town and is the only stone church in Rawlins. The building passed to Victory Baptist Church, which kept the old name.',
  'Stone walls and Gothic windows. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'In use as Victory Baptist Church, 3rd and Cedar Streets (the geocoder gives 202 W. Cedar Street). View from the street; services and visits not checked.',
  [W('France Memorial United Presbyterian Church')],brick='no'),
N('parco-inn-sinclair','Parco Inn and the company town of Parco','Sinclair','wy','hotel','standing',41.77957,-107.11539,'site','view','drive','hotels','1924–1925',
  'Frank Kistler\'s Producers and Refiners Oil Company (PARCO) built Parco in 1924-25 to house the workers at its refinery, all to one design by Fisher and Fisher of Denver: Spanish Colonial, with unpainted stucco, coloured clay-tile roofs and iron balconies. The sixty-room Parco Inn, which opened on May 31, 1925, fills a whole block on the plaza between two bell towers; its Fountain Room restaurant kept live trout in the pool. Sinclair\'s company took over in 1934 and renamed the town in 1942, and sold most of it to residents in 1967. The inn has been privately owned since.',
  'The inn\'s twin bell towers and tiled roofs dominate the plaza, with the theatre and recreation hall to its west and brick bungalows on North Eighth Street. The marker says the lobby had painted cedar beams and stained-glass skylights; whether the inn is open, empty or in use today was not checked. Interior not checked.',
  'An oil company\'s Spanish mission town on the Wyoming plains.',
  'The inn is private. See it from Lincoln Avenue; the state marker is in the median near 4th Street. The Parco Historic District takes in the town centre (Register, 1987).',
  [W('Parco Historic District'),L('HMdb: The Parco Inn','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=89715'),L('National Register form: Parco Historic District','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/87000918_text')],brick='?'),
N('carbon-ghost-town-wy','Carbon ghost town and cemetery','Medicine Bow','wy','town','ruins',41.85197,-106.37874,'site','ask','drive','cemeteries','1868–1902',
  'The Union Pacific opened Carbon in 1868 to dig coal for its locomotives, with miners brought in from Missouri; its first mine was working by that September. It grew to a town of churches, a bank, a school, an opera house and a racetrack over seven mines, some tunnelled under the town. Mining fell off by the late 1880s, the railroad moved its line, the people went to Hanna, and the last resident died in 1912. The cemetery, on the Register since 2011, holds 239 marked and 98 unmarked graves, including mine-explosion victims of 1903 and 1908.',
  'The cemetery: a fenced five acres on the west side of the county road with headstones, rock cairns and the Finnish miners\' iron markers with flat plates, most of the lettering gone. Just south, the townsite holds many stone foundations; east of the road the old racetrack oval shows faintly.',
  'A coal camp the railroad made and then left.',
  'County Road 115 turns south off U.S. 30 at the unmarked Como railroad crossing, 9.5 miles west of Medicine Bow; the cemetery is about five miles south. WyoHistory says the road is seasonal and crosses private land through two gates, and that vehicle access is arranged by appointment through the Hanna Basin Museum. The Register form says the townsite is under separate private ownership from the cemetery. Ask first.',
  [W('Carbon Cemetery'),W('Carbon, Wyoming'),L('WyoHistory: Carbon field trip','https://www.wyohistory.org/field-trips/carbon-wyoming'),L('National Register form: Carbon Cemetery','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/10001048_text')],brick='?'),
N('hanna-basin-museum','Hanna Community Hall (Hanna Basin Museum)','Hanna','wy','civic','standing',41.86907,-106.56465,'exact','open','drive','','1890',
  'John Linden built this as a saloon for the coal miners in 1890 and later moved it into the centre of Hanna with the Union Pacific Coal Company\'s leave. Under Prohibition it became a pool hall, then the town\'s community hall after the owner\'s death in the 1920s; the company paid to renovate it in 1931. It is now the Hanna Basin Museum.',
  'A one-story clapboard hall with a hipped roof and a small bracketed canopy over the door. Inside, exhibits on the coal camps: clothing, tools and photographs, and the place to ask about Carbon.',
  '',
  'Hanna Basin Museum, 502 Front Street. The county visitors\' site gives summer hours Friday to Sunday 1 to 5 p.m., winter Friday 1 to 5 and by appointment (read November 2026). Street parking.',
  [W('Hanna Basin Museum'),L(CCVC+': Hanna Basin Museum','https://www.snowmobileinwyoming.com/things-to-do/museums/hanna-basin-museum')],brick='no',hours='Summer: Friday to Sunday 1 to 5 p.m. Winter: Friday 1 to 5, and by appointment'),
N('medicine-bow-depot','Union Pacific depot, Medicine Bow (Medicine Bow Museum)','Medicine Bow','wy','rail','standing',41.89522,-106.20042,'exact','open','drive','stations','1913',
  'A one-story frame depot with a hipped roof on wide bracketed eaves and a bay for the agent to watch for trains; the west end held the waiting room, office and baggage room, the east end the agent\'s family. The county visitors\' page says it was built in November 1913 after fire took the first depot that year; Wikipedia gives 1912-1919. Trains stopped using it in 1981 and the town took it over; it has been the Medicine Bow Museum since 1983.',
  'Beside it: a Union Pacific caboose, the hunting cabin of Owen Wister (author of The Virginian) brought here from Jackson Hole, and a 1939 monument to him made of petrified wood. The Fossil Cabin now stands on the museum grounds too.',
  '',
  'Medicine Bow Museum, 405 Lincoln Highway (U.S. 30). Open daily in summer and hunting season and part-time in winter, per the county visitors\' page (read November 2026); phone 307-379-2383. Fees not checked.',
  [W('Medicine Bow station'),L(CCVC+': Medicine Bow Museum','https://www.wyomingcarboncounty.com/things-to-do/museums/medicine-bow-museum')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('fossil-cabin-medicine-bow','Fossil Cabin','Medicine Bow','wy','amuse','standing',41.8955,-106.2007,'area','view','drive','','1932',
  'Thomas Boylan, a homesteader near Como Bluff, where dinosaur bones had been found since the 1870s, spent years collecting them and in 1932 built a 29-by-19-foot cabin out of 5,796 of them (Wikipedia; Cowboy State Daily says more than 6,000) as a gas station sideshow on the Lincoln Highway. Ripley\'s called it "the world\'s oldest cabin" in 1938. Traffic left with Interstate 80 in the 1960s. On May 13, 2026 the 52-ton cabin was braced, wrapped and moved seven miles to a new home beside the Medicine Bow Museum.',
  'Bone-studded walls in pale mortar. In October 2026 the cabin stood behind a fence while the museum replaced the roof, sealed cracks and waited for rebuilt windows; it could be seen only from the fence, the inside was empty, and no reopening date was set.',
  'A cabin built of dinosaur bones, rolled seven miles down the Lincoln Highway in 2026.',
  'On the Medicine Bow Museum grounds, 405 Lincoln Highway. Look from behind the fence until the museum reopens it (Cowboy State Daily, October 18, 2026); its old site at Como Bluff is now empty.',
  [W('Fossil Cabin'),L('Cowboy State Daily: Fossil Cabin moved to Medicine Bow (May 2026)','https://cowboystatedaily.com/2026/05/15/wyomings-fossil-cabin-worlds-oldest-building-moved-to-medicine-bow-museum/'),L('Cowboy State Daily: Fossil Cabin off limits (October 2026)','https://cowboystatedaily.com/2026/10/18/medicine-bows-worlds-oldest-cabin-hits-snag-that-might-delay-re-opening/')],brick='no'),
N('virginian-hotel-medicine-bow','Virginian Hotel','Medicine Bow','wy','hotel','standing',41.8959,-106.20072,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1901–1911',
  'August Grimm, Medicine Bow\'s first mayor, and George Plummer began the hotel in 1901 and opened it on September 30, 1911, named, it is thought, for Owen Wister\'s novel set here. It was said to be the biggest hotel between Denver and Salt Lake City, and it brought the town its first electric lights and sewer. The three-and-a-half-story building is of concrete block made on the site with sand from the Medicine Bow River. In 2023 the owner, Vernon Scott, told Cowboy State Daily he bought it from his mother, who bought it from his grandfather.',
  'Victorian gold and burgundy wallpaper, velvet drapes, pressed-tin ceilings 12 feet high, brass beds and steam radiators; the Owen Wister Dining Room, and the Shiloh Saloon with bullet holes in the bar.',
  '',
  'A working hotel, restaurant and bar on the old Lincoln Highway (U.S. 30). Cowboy State Daily said in October 2023 that it is open all year. Current hours not checked.',
  [W('Virginian Hotel (Medicine Bow, Wyoming)'),L('Cowboy State Daily: The Virginian Hotel (2023)','https://cowboystatedaily.com/2023/10/29/the-virginian-hotel-has-been-a-wyoming-and-western-icon-for-more-than-a-century')],brick='no'),
N('medicine-bow-airway-field','Medicine Bow airway beacon field','Medicine Bow','wy','landmark','standing',41.8865,-106.1884,'area','view','drive','','1929',
  'An emergency landing field on the lighted Transcontinental Airway between Salt Lake and Omaha, built a mile west and moved here in 1929 as Site 32 SL-O. A 51-foot steel tower carried the airway beacon (a 36-inch Crouse-Hinds lamp from about 1934) and green course lights, and a concrete arrow on the ground below pointed the way to the next beacon. In 2012 it was part of the Medicine Bow airport, owned by the town and the State of Wyoming.',
  'The steel beacon tower in the north-west corner of the field with the concrete arrow at its foot, a small 22-by-14-foot frame powerhouse (later the teletype office), and orange-and-white wooden boundary markers along the fence.',
  'Where the night airmail pilots looked for the next light.',
  'Part of the Medicine Bow airport, about nine-tenths of a mile south-east of town off County Road 1, owned by the Town of Medicine Bow and the State of Wyoming (Register form, 2012). See it from the road; do not go onto the airfield.',
  [W('Medicine Bow Airport'),L('National Register form: Site 32 SL-O Intermediate Field','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/12000054_text')],brick='no'),
N('elk-mountain-hotel','Elk Mountain Hotel','Elk Mountain','wy','hotel','standing',41.68746,-106.41254,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1905',
  'John Evans, owner of the Elk Mountain Saloon, built this two-story frame hotel in 1905 on the Medicine Bow River beside the Garden Spot Pavilion, a dance hall of 1880 that drew Lawrence Welk, Tommy Dorsey and Louis Armstrong and people from up to 200 miles away. Evans served the mining trade and later hunters and tourists, and sold out in 1947. The pavilion was pulled down in the early 2000s; the hotel was renovated in 2000-2002.',
  'A two-story porch on the narrow front, guest rooms upstairs and public rooms below. Interior not checked beyond the hotel\'s own pictures of its lobby and dining room.',
  '',
  'A hotel and restaurant in the town of Elk Mountain. In November 2026 its site said it was "hibernating" for the winter and taking bookings for 2027; phone 307-348-7774.',
  [W('Elk Mountain Hotel'),L('Elk Mountain Hotel','https://www.elkmountainhotel.com')],brick='no'),
N('hotel-wolf-saratoga','Hotel Wolf','Saratoga','wy','hotel','standing',41.45449,-106.80745,'exact','open','drive','hotels','1893',
  'Frederick Wolf, a German immigrant who had sold liquor in Wyoming, built this two-story brick hotel for about $6,000 in 1893. It opened for dining with a New Year\'s Eve banquet that year and took its first guests on January 10, 1894, serving stage passengers bound for Encampment and investors in the copper mines. His widow sold it in 1912. The Campbell family has owned it since 1977.',
  'An L-shaped brick building with a steep gabled roof, pointed dormers and a corbelled brick band with shallow arches over two ground-floor windows. Still a hotel, restaurant and saloon.',
  '',
  'A working hotel and restaurant at 101 E. Bridge Avenue, downtown Saratoga. Hours not checked.',
  [W('Hotel Wolf'),L('Hotel Wolf: history','https://www.wolfhotel.com/history')],brick='yes'),
N('grand-encampment-museum','Grand Encampment Museum and tramway towers','Encampment','wy','industry','standing',41.2038,-106.78867,'exact','open','drive','','1902–1908',
  'Copper found in the Sierra Madre in 1897 made Grand Encampment a boom town. From 1902 to 1908 the Ferris-Haggarty tramway carried ore 16 miles from the mine to the Boston-Wyoming smelter in 840 buckets on 375 towers, the longest aerial ore tram in the world. Falling copper prices and smelter fires ended it. Town residents founded this museum in the 1960s; it holds more than a dozen old buildings.',
  'A few original tramway towers stand on the grounds holding a short length of cable with ore buckets. Also a fire lookout tower to climb, the Saratoga and Encampment Railway caboose, and the old buildings filled with timber, mining and farm things.',
  'The towers of what was the longest ore tramway in the world.',
  'Grand Encampment Museum, 807 Barnett Avenue. County visitors\' page (read November 2026): summer Tuesday to Saturday 9 to 5 and Sunday noon to 4; winter most weekdays 10 to 4, call first, 307-327-5308. Fees not checked.',
  [L('WyoHistory: Encampment, Wyoming','https://www.wyohistory.org/encyclopedia/encampment-wyoming'),L('WyoFile: Encampment\'s aerial tram','https://wyofile.com/encampments-aerial-tram/'),L(CCVC+': Grand Encampment Museum','https://www.wyomingcarboncounty.com/things-to-do/museums/grand-encampment-museum'),L('Grand Encampment Museum','https://www.gemuseum.com')],brick='?',hours='Summer: Tuesday to Saturday 9 to 5, Sunday noon to 4. Winter: most weekdays 10 to 4, call first, 307-327-5308'),
N('grand-encampment-opera-house','Grand Encampment Opera House','Encampment','wy','civic','standing',41.20794,-106.7941,'exact','view','drive','','1902',
  'Built by the town\'s people in 1902, at the height of the copper boom, as a meeting place. Encampment\'s editor Earle Clemens set Charles Winter\'s words for "Wyoming" to music the next year, and it became the state song. The opera house is still the town\'s community hall.',
  'A boom-town hall still in use: melodramas are put on here during the Woodchoppers Jamboree in mid-June and at other times. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'At 622 Rankin Avenue; a community building. See it from the street; inside only during events (WyoHistory gives 307-327-5501).',
  [L('WyoHistory: Encampment, Wyoming (field trips)','https://www.wyohistory.org/encyclopedia/encampment-wyoming')],brick='?'),
N('tom-sun-ranch','Tom Sun Ranch (Martin\'s Cove visitors\' centre)','Alcova','wy','landmark','standing',42.44333,-107.21833,'site','open','drive','','1872',
  'Tom Sun (Thomas de Beau Soleil), a French-Canadian frontiersman, started a cattle ranch by Devil\'s Gate on the Oregon and Mormon trails in 1872; a Cheyenne paper held it up in 1882 as the model of a Wyoming ranch. It became a National Historic Landmark in 1960 and stayed with the Sun family for four generations, until the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints bought it in 1996. The Church uses the original ranch house as the Martin\'s Cove visitors\' centre, for the handcart company caught here by snow in 1856.',
  'The ranch buildings, stabilized and repaired, with exhibits and a film in the old ranch house; a trail leads to Devil\'s Gate. Wikipedia notes new parking lots and landscaping round them.',
  '',
  'Martin\'s Cove Visitors\' Center, 47600 W. WY-220, about 5 miles south-west of Independence Rock, run by the Church (National Park Service trail page). Hours not checked.',
  [W('Tom Sun Ranch'),L('National Park Service: Martin\'s Cove','https://www.nps.gov/places/000/martins-cove.htm')],brick='?'),
]
